From 6e5fdd71fe23260b52dac3da78010b9c27e894c6 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: cruz <cruz@tbu.net>
Date: Thu, 27 Aug 2026 11:33:28 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 112/206] fix(shorebird): the pool load counter, and two checks
 that release builds threw away

InstructionCountForLoadStoreFromOffset measured PrepareLargeOffset. Two of the
six object pool kinds do not use it: LoadWordFromPoolIndex and
StoreWordToPoolIndex have a ladder of their own whose last rung is always
movz+movk, where PrepareLargeOffset goes through LoadImmediate and drops the
movk when the low sixteen bits are zero. So the count came out one low at
offset 2^24, and the editor -- which locates the start of a sequence by
counting backwards from its last instruction -- would have started a rewrite
inside the sequence. The recon agreed with the emitters all along: their folded
Address::Offset ladder is 1 / 2 / 3 and is 3 at 2^24. The counter now follows
that ladder for kEightBytes + Address::Offset and keeps measuring for every
other size and address type, which really is PrepareLargeOffset.

PadLoadStore's two checks were ASSERTs, so they existed in no build that runs
them: PadLoadStore is gated on FLAG_precompiled_mode, and the only builds that
open that gate are release and product. Both are RELEASE_ASSERTs now, and
turning them on found the thing an ASSERT could not: AssembleReplacement wraps
PadLoadStore around emitters that wrap it around themselves, so every rewritten
access in gen_snapshot ran two nested PadLoadStores and the outer one measured
an already-padded emission. The bytes were right -- padding is idempotent --
but the invariant was unmeetable. PadLoadStore is now re-entrancy aware: the
outermost call measures, checks and pads, and a nested one just runs its body.
The outer wrapper cannot simply be dropped, because with the gate shut it is
the only thing doing the padding.

Not changed: the pair kind. LoadDoubleWordFromPoolIndex takes `add TMP, PP,
#imm` for any immediate an add can hold where PrepareLargeOffset insists the low
twelve bits fit an imm7, so the two disagree from index 62 up -- which is not a
corner, flow_graph_compiler_arm64.cc emits it for every AOT instance call, and
making the new check unconditional aborts gen_snapshot on the fixture. That
emitter is upstream code this fork has not diverged from, so it says nothing
about which ladder theirs follows; guessing would be invention. The release
check is applied only where the ladder is known, and the case stays pinned in
ObjectPoolEditor_CountFollowsPrepareLargeOffset.

The two tests that pinned the counter to LoadFromOffset now pin it to the pool
emitter, which is the emission anything ever asks it about.

Verified: verify_all.sh 16 ran / 0 failed, verify_reproduction.sh 28 artifacts
/ 0 failures, run_vm_tests 157 passed. Negative controls, each with ninja at
exit 0: breaking the last rung to 2 aborts Arm64PadLoadStore at the
RELEASE_ASSERT in the Release build and reddens two more tests; the same break
with the check written as ASSERT passes silently, which is the point; breaking
the first rung aborts the Product gen_snapshot on a real compile.

Co-Authored-By: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>

